ass=3D"prose-ui overflow-x-auto"><h2 id=3D"pick-the-right-method-before-you=
-run-anything">Pick the right method before you run anything</h2><p>FFmpeg =
has three separate mechanisms for concatenating videos, and choosing the wr=
ong one wastes time. Here's the decision table:</p><table><thead><tr class=
=3D""><td class=3D"">Situation</td><td class=3D"">Method</td><td class=3D""=
>Re-encodes?</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=3D""><td class=3D"">Same cod=
ec, resolution, frame rate</td><td class=3D"">Concat demuxer</td><td class=
=3D"">No</td></tr><tr class=3D""><td class=3D"">Different codecs or resolut=
ions</td><td class=3D"">Concat filter</td><td class=3D"">Yes</td></tr><tr c=
lass=3D""><td class=3D"">Existing <code>.ts</code> / HLS segments</td><td c=
lass=3D"">Concat protocol</td><td class=3D"">No</td></tr><tr class=3D""><td=
 class=3D"">Variable frame rate clips (phone video)</td><td class=3D"">Conc=
at filter</td><td class=3D"">Yes</td></tr><tr class=3D""><td class=3D"">Aud=
io files only</td><td class=3D"">Concat demuxer or filter</td><td class=3D"=
">Depends</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If your clips came from the same sour=
ce (same camera, same export preset), start with the demuxer. It's fast bec=
ause it copies bytes instead of re-encoding. If anything doesn't match, swi=
tch to the filter.</p><p>The <a href=3D"https://renderio.dev/blogs/ffmpeg-m=
erge-videos">FFmpeg merge videos guide</a> covers the full workflow for com=
bining clips. This guide goes deeper on the concat mechanisms themselves an=
d how to diagnose when things break.</p><h2 id=3D"the-concat-demuxer">The c=
oncat demuxer</h2><p>The demuxer reads a list of files and joins them witho=
ut touching the encoded data. It's the fastest option when it applies.</p><=
